### Reacting to application state changes[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/application-configuration.html#reacting-to-application-state-changes "Link to this heading")
Use these functions to react to application state changes:  
| Function  | Description  |  
| --- | --- |  
| `Application::onSuspend[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#a8703cd9f173c924b96ea39ef207296cd)`  |  Kanzi calls this function in a frame where no rendering was performed, to determine whether to suspend the application and for what duration. The default implementation calculates appropriate timeout based on active animations, timers, and resources waiting for deployment. Use this function to customize application suspension.  |  
| `Application::onPause[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#a2027738626a9cb3bccf955bf3184eb44)`  | Kanzi calls this function when the application main loop enters the `MainLoopState::Paused[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11119.html#ae35554056f5fa4e74b1d5d5921bf8ea4ae99180abf47a8b3a856e0bcb2656990a)` state.  |  
| `Application::onResume[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#ad711348d8da0bdc6e6bd2acf7cbf33fe)`  | Kanzi calls this function when the application main loop returns from the `MainLoopState::Paused[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11119.html#ae35554056f5fa4e74b1d5d5921bf8ea4ae99180abf47a8b3a856e0bcb2656990a)` to the `MainLoopState::Running[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11119.html#ae35554056f5fa4e74b1d5d5921bf8ea4a5bda814c4aedb126839228f1a3d92f09)` state.  |  
| `Application::onShutdown[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#a76a0e129d5996bc1704166ce054538e9)`  | Kanzi calls this function immediately before the application uninitialization.  |  
## Modifying the main loop logic[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/application-configuration.html#modifying-the-main-loop-logic "Link to this heading")
The main loop consists of a sequence of stages, where each stage consists of a sequence of tasks. A task is any callable item, including functions, function objects, and lambdas.
C++

```
using Task = function<void(chrono::nanoseconds)>;

```
Copy to clipboard
Java

```
public interface Task {
    void handle(Duration lastFrameDuration);
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Kotlin

```
interface Task {
    fun handle(lastFrameDuration: Duration?)
}

```
Copy to clipboard
When Kanzi executes a task, it passes the last frame duration as an argument to the task.
These are the default stages in Kanzi:
  * In the input stage Kanzi handles the input events.
  * In the user stage Kanzi handles update logic: deploys resources, loads kzb files and prefabs, and executes your task dispatcher and timer tasks.
  * In the animate stage Kanzi ticks the active animations.
  * In the layout stage Kanzi performs layout the node tree.
  * In the render stage Kanzi renders the node tree.
  * In the present stage Kanzi presents the node tree.


This diagram shows the control flow of the Kanzi main loop.
[![../../_images/main-loop.svg](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/main-loop.svg)](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/main-loop.svg)
In an application you can remove, replace, or insert tasks in any main loop scheduler stage. See `MainLoopScheduler[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html)`.
### Adding recurring tasks[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/application-configuration.html#adding-recurring-tasks "Link to this heading")
When you want Kanzi to execute a task every frame, you can add it to any stage of the main loop using `MainLoopScheduler::appendTask[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#ac04444ddeaddd36eb39fb873f3725a02)` and `MainLoopScheduler::prependTask[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#ad25433d89afd701f619b4d45c26b2346)`, with option `MainLoopScheduler::TaskRecurrence::Recurring[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#aba0f144351f8dd75db0fc404e05546d5a582ff616b727759a2208c65b27b0d309)`.
For example, you can use a recurring task to call the update function of an external library:
C++

```
void onStartup() override
{
    // From the main loop Animate stage, tick an external physics engine.
    getMainLoopScheduler().appendTask(AnimateStage,
                                      kzMakeFixedString("TickPhysics"),
                                      MainLoopScheduler::TaskRecurrence::Recurring,
                                      [this](auto deltaTime)
                                      {
                                          this->getPhysicsEngine()->tick(deltaTime);
                                      });
}

```
